  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH TUESDAY:\n\nForecast remains generally on track. Warm air advection aloft \ncontinued early this morning which will have an impact on \nforecast high temperatures today. Readings will top out in the \nlow to upper 40s. A few spots may hit 50 degrees, mainly in \nnorthwest Wisconsin. These values are 8 to 15 degrees above \nnormal for mid-November. Winds will turn southerly at 5 to 10 \nmph as surface high pressure drifts off to the east. Cloud cover\nwill increase tonight which may help buoy low temps in the \nmiddle 20s to middle 30s. A tightening surface pressure gradient\nwill work to keep winds in the 3 to 8 mph range overnight, \nwhich will in turn keep the boundary layer mixed. If clouds and \nlight, but not calm, winds do not arrive, the current forecast \nlows will end up around 10-15 degrees too warm.\n\nTemperatures nudge a little warmer on Monday with highs in the \nmiddle 40s to low 50s, around 10-20 degrees above normal. The \nwarmer than normal trend will continue through the rest of the \nweek.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY:\n\nA pronounced pattern change will begin Wednesday. A longwave \ntrough over the western US will propagate eastward into the \nPlains. Poleward moisture transport will increase over the \nMississippi River Valley to around the 90th percentile of \nclimatology with values ranging from 0.5-0.9 inches by early \nWednesday evening. Surface low pressure will develop over the \nnorthern Plains and propagate northeastward into northwest \nOntario during that time. Precipitation chances across the \nNorthland will tick upward slightly. There is expected to be \nconsiderable dry air in the lower 1/3 of the atmosphere which \nwill evaporate light precipitation. Forcing for ascent will be \nmaximized near and north of the low track with areas north of \nUS-2 seeing the greatest chance for measurable rain. With the \nwarm air, clouds, and precipitation, both Wednesday and Thursday\nhave a potential to set new warm low temperature records at a \nfew of our climate sites. See the Climate section below for more\ndetails.\n\nThat system scoots northeastward out of our area Thursday night\nwith ridging building over the region briefly. Friday will \nremain above normal temperaturewise. A potentially more \nsubstantive storm system will take shape over the Plains Friday \nand move toward our region for Friday night and Saturday, \npossibly lingering into next Sunday.\n\nThe overall confidence in a more active and progressive pattern\nis high. However, details regarding precipitation timing, \namounts, and other aspects encompass a wide range of \npossibilities. Some of the ensemble members are painting a swath\nof accumulating snow over portions of the Northland from that \nsystem. The locations are quite diverse among the membership, so\nconfidence is low overall. That being said, there will be ample\ncold air upstream over the Canadian Prairies and a few of the \nmembers of each family yielded a stripe of snow with yesterday \nmorning's run, too. Definitely a situation which bears \nmonitoring over the next few days to see if some consistency \ntakes shape. Temperatures are expected to trend closer to normal\nby next Sunday with highs in the low 30s to low 40s.",
